# Complete Module Delivery (Company Gold Standard)

When the user shares a **module name** plus **minimum details** (fields, CRUD roles, optional auto-code / FKs), deliver a **complete module**: Database + API + Frontend — not a partial slice — unless they explicitly ask for one layer only.

## Source of truth

1. `Requirements/documents/Xenasys_Complete_Module_Delivery_Contract.md` — delivery checklist + Company inventory  
2. `Requirements/documents/Xenasys_Company_Module_Micro_Pin_Playbook.md` — micro-pins to clone  
3. `Requirements/documents/Xenasys_Task_Matrix.md` — append audit-log row before coding  

## Minimum user input (enough to start)

- Module UI name  
- Field list (name, type, required)  
- Who can create/read/update/delete  
- Optional: auto-code, soft vs hard delete, parent FK, unique rules  

Do **not** wait for screen mockups if Category/Company pattern already covers the UX.

## Must clone from Company

- Viewport-locked listing (`fill` + sticky pagination)  
- DataTable spreadsheet: no `#` column; header filters + asc/desc  
- SideDrawer create/view/edit; RHF+Zod; disabled View chrome  
- `ConfirmDialog` for delete (soft `IsActive=false` by default)  
- Claims + `ClaimGate`; active-only + `shape=list|detail` APIs  
- Audit columns last; `AuditClock` PKT; UI business names (no DB jargon)  
- EF Core LINQ (not SPs) unless user explicitly requires stored procedures  

## Persistence note

Xenasys Company baseline uses EF Core, not application stored procedures. Default new modules the same way.
